WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.120
All right.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.120
Welcome to the program.

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:03.199
We are back from vacation.

00:00:03.199 --> 00:00:06.049
We'll have a lot of new stuff happening for
you this year

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:11.490
and where I want to start today is I want
to talk about the future of the United States,

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:15.959
but not, I mean the Trump is part of it, but
that's not really what this story is about.

00:00:15.959 --> 00:00:21.320
I know that now when we talk about the future
of the country, very often it relates to Donald

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.320
Trump.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:28.990
Has Donald Trump irreparably done a, B or
C or how will we recover from Def that Donald

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:29.990
Trump has done?

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.570
That's not really what this is about.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:36.760
This is, this is a bigger picture story, but
importantly, it's also not about predicting

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.969
the future in any particular sense.

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:43.039
Humans are notoriously bad at predicting the
future.

00:00:43.039 --> 00:00:44.160
It's not just economists.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.589
I'm talking about, we've talked about how
economists are bad at predicting the future

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:52.739
of the economy, but with change happening
more and more quickly, predictions about what

00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:56.489
humanity will be like or what the United States
will be like in 50 years.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:01:01.149
In a hundred years, in 500 years, it's increasingly
pointless to do that.

00:01:01.149 --> 00:01:02.149
Type of thing.

00:01:02.149 --> 00:01:07.510
Any predictions about this will definitely
be, or this will never be, these are not useful

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:09.000
predictions to make.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.030
So for example, when Donald Trump said during
his last state of the union address that the

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:18.730
United States will never be a communist country
and, or maybe he used the word socialist and

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:21.400
everybody cheer, Republicans cheered.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.760
Other than that being a really ignorant thing
to say because there's really no risk of that

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.590
happening in the immediate, like the idea
that the country is on the verge of becoming

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:31.750
a socialist country.

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:34.950
That's not the case to say something will
never happen.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:39.120
Makes very little sense because it's 500 years
is a long time.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.480
A hundred years is a long time.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:41.480
Fair enough.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:48.170
I think we understand that what this story
is about is about the future of the American

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:49.360
political system.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:55.750
Now, there is no doubt that many of us believe
elements of the current system are unstable

00:01:55.750 --> 00:02:02.870
and precarious and at some point it could
be natural to think the system will collapse

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:07.710
or evolve and many people feel that there
is an inevitability to that.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.879
So I'll give you some specific examples.

00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:16.680
Marxists have long held that eventually the
proletariat is going to revolt against the

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:17.680
bourgeoisie.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:22.790
In capitalist countries like the United States,
the system will collapse and in its place

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:26.370
will be installed a Marxist system of government.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:27.370
Okay?

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:33.349
That's an example of a Marxist idea of the
inevitability of what will be the future political

00:02:33.349 --> 00:02:34.349
system.

00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:40.930
Free market capitalists on the hand who believe
that the ideas of social democracy are fraught,

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.760
untenable, uh, bad ideas.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:49.450
The free market capitalists often imagine
that as those ideas fail, be it single payer

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:54.700
health care or whatever, society will move
more and more in the direction of total free

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.519
market capitalism.

00:02:56.519 --> 00:03:02.920
More generally, history has shown us that
nation States often don't last forever, right?

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:06.470
Whether we're talking about the fall of Rome
or the Ottoman empire or there are many other

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.249
examples, and it is true.

00:03:08.249 --> 00:03:10.249
A country can be destroyed by war.

00:03:10.249 --> 00:03:15.670
A country can be taken over, it can be annexed,
it can be divided up into multiple smaller

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:20.519
countries as we saw happen during the 1990s
in Europe, for example.

00:03:20.519 --> 00:03:25.870
But it's important for any good activists
to understand that it's called the status

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:33.180
quo for a reason and things can basically
remain as they are and this is actually a

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.749
desired outcome.

00:03:34.749 --> 00:03:40.249
This is a goal for many elements of society
that wield influence, and I'll explain what

00:03:40.249 --> 00:03:43.169
I mean because this is really important stuff
to understand.

00:03:43.169 --> 00:03:49.260
We look at healthcare in the United States
and we look at medical bankruptcy, the increased

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:54.319
cost of health insurance, the terrible plans
that costs, you know, 500 bucks a month for

00:03:54.319 --> 00:04:00.499
a $10,000 per year deductible or whatever,
and we think this can't go on forever.

00:04:00.499 --> 00:04:05.060
Like eventually this has to collapse, but
maybe not.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:11.879
There are very powerful interests that want
the system to basically remain this way, but

00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:15.019
they recognize that it's not sustainable indefinitely.

00:04:15.019 --> 00:04:18.959
In other words, we say this is not indefinitely
sustainable.

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:24.099
Those who want the current system also recognize
it's not indefinitely sustainable if the trajectory

00:04:24.099 --> 00:04:31.729
continues and they can choose to do just enough
to keep basically the same system going.

00:04:31.729 --> 00:04:33.710
I'll give you another example.

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:39.319
We look at the cost of college, which has
skyrocketed some colleges costing 60 70 $80,000

00:04:39.319 --> 00:04:40.319
a year.

00:04:40.319 --> 00:04:42.550
Maybe my numbers are outdated and it's even
more.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:46.139
We project out that growth and we say, this
can't go on.

00:04:46.139 --> 00:04:50.330
This is a system that's going to implode on
itself because college will soon cost hundreds

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:52.169
of thousands of dollars a year.

00:04:52.169 --> 00:04:56.110
People won't be able to afford it, or they'll
take out student loans, which they will never

00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:00.680
be able to pay because wages are not increasing
in parallel with the cost of college, so it'll

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:06.370
collapse and we'll start over hopefully with
something better or the powers that be, which

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:13.370
include colleges, student lending, institutions
and others can tweak what we have put in some

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:18.120
subsidies, raise wages a little bit to make
expensive college degrees just slightly more

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:25.129
worth it to basically perpetuate the system
that we have now have huge profits for lenders,

00:05:25.129 --> 00:05:30.090
schools and students who get degrees with
which they can't really get ahead and take

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:34.099
decades to pay the loans off if they ever
are able to pay them off.

00:05:34.099 --> 00:05:41.180
The entire point here is the assumption that
an unsustainable system will collapse, that

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:47.370
then we can change into something we like
ignores that the status quo is not only appealing

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:53.129
to many systems of power, but that it will
be a great motivator to them to fix little

00:05:53.129 --> 00:05:58.110
things to avoid the collapse, just to perpetuate
the status quo.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:02.300
This is how they will throw bones to the masses
every once in a while.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:08.150
This is part of the same system that prevents
people from being able to afford really getting

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:12.639
out there and protesting big time for massive
systemic change.

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:19.680
The system works to keep people so day to
day when it comes to their salaries that they

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:24.599
can't take time off to go and protest, but
then if things get too hot, they will throw

00:06:24.599 --> 00:06:26.789
us a bone here or there.

00:06:26.789 --> 00:06:30.650
Things can basically stay the same or close
to it for a long time.

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:36.100
And as I've said before, it's always harder
to win when you want to do stuff and the other

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.759
side wants to keep things the same.

00:06:38.759 --> 00:06:44.949
But knowing this better arms us to be activists
we're working against, we're working against,

00:06:44.949 --> 00:06:47.919
and my pronunciation was a little bit incorrect
there.

00:06:47.919 --> 00:06:53.759
We're working against not just people who
want to change things for the worse for the

00:06:53.759 --> 00:06:59.159
most number of people, but there are huge
forces that we are fighting that merely want

00:06:59.159 --> 00:07:03.110
to tweak the edges to basically maintain the
status quo.

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:07.580
That includes corporate powers and it includes
moneyed interests, so understand that the

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:13.210
collapse is not inevitable because a strategy
is doing just enough to avoid the collapse,

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:18.449
but basically perpetuate the status quo and
a big part of this is economics and we're

00:07:18.449 --> 00:07:18.839
going to dig into some economics next.

